# Commit 10: Register AMQP placeholders as real SAP externalized parameters

**Branch**: `Work-on-Parameters-and-Remaining-Components`
**Scope**: `packages/compiler` (the `@cpi-ai/compiler` package)
**Audience**: same as commits 1-9 -- written for both future Claude sessions
and human contributors, explaining the evidence and reasoning behind each
decision.

---

## 1. What broke (confirmed by inspecting the uploaded generated ZIP)

Commit 9 taught `AmqpAdapter` to accept a `"{{PlaceholderName}}"`-shaped
string for `host`/`port`/`credentialName` as an alternative to a real
literal value, on the theory that SAP's own reference export uses exactly
this syntax and is a validly-importable flow. That theory was **half
right**: the placeholder syntax is necessary, but not sufficient on its
own. Inspecting the user-uploaded `flow (28).zip` (this compiler's own
output) confirmed it:

- `AMQP to HTTP Flow.iflw` correctly contains
  `<key>host</key><value>{{AMQP_HOST}}</value>` (and the same for
  `destinationName`/`port`/`credentialName`).
- But `parameters.prop` is just `#<timestamp>` (no parameter values), and
  `parameters.propdef` is just `<parameters><param_references/></parameters>`
  (no parameter definitions, no references at all).

SAP Integration Suite still reported the exact same three errors
(`Attribute 'Host' is mandatory`, `Attribute 'Credential Name' is
mandatory`, `Enter a value between 1 and 65535`) -- because a `{{...}}`
string in a messageFlow property is only exempted from SAP's design-time
mandatory/format validation when it is **also** registered as a real
parameter in `parameters.propdef` (with a matching `parameters.prop`
entry). Without that registration, SAP evaluates the field as a literal
(invalid) string, `{{AMQP_HOST}}` included.

---

## 2. Reference vs. generated comparison (as requested)

### AMQP XML -- reference (`amqp_reference.zip`, `MessageFlow_882`)
```xml
<ifl:property><key>host</key><value>{{EMHOST}}</value></ifl:property>
<ifl:property><key>port</key><value>{{EMPORT}}</value></ifl:property>
<ifl:property><key>credentialName</key><value>{{EMUser}}</value></ifl:property>
```

### AMQP XML -- our generated ZIP (`flow (28).zip`, `MessageFlow_4`)
```xml
<key>host</key><value>{{AMQP_HOST}}</value>
<key>port</key><value>{{AMQP_PORT}}</value>
<key>credentialName</key><value>{{AMQP_CREDENTIAL}}</value>
```
(Placeholder *names* differ because the user's AI JSON chose its own names
-- that's expected and fine; the shape is correct.)

### parameters.prop -- reference (excerpt)
```
EMAUTH=Transport_OAuth2
EMConcurrent=1
EMHOST=enterprise-messaging-messaging-gateway.cfapps.eu10.hana.ondemand.com
EMPORT=443
EMUser=EventMesh_Oauth
QUEUNAME=queue\:sap/s4/EMD/Batch_D3_InitialLoad_Queue
... (38 total parameters across every externalized adapter in that flow)
```

### parameters.propdef -- reference (AMQP-relevant excerpt)
```xml
<parameter><key/><name>EMHOST</name><type>xsd:string</type><isRequired>false</isRequired><constraint/><description/><additionalMetadata/></parameter>
<parameter><key/><name>EMPORT</name><type>xsd:integer</type><isRequired>false</isRequired><constraint/><description/><additionalMetadata/></parameter>
<parameter><key/><name>EMUser</name><type>xsd:string</type><isRequired>false</isRequired><constraint/><description/><additionalMetadata/></parameter>
...
<param_references>
  <reference attribute_category="EventMesh" attribute_id="/attrId::host" attribute_uilabel="" param_key="EMHOST"/>
  <reference attribute_category="EventMesh" attribute_id="/attrId::port" attribute_uilabel="" param_key="EMPORT"/>
  <reference attribute_category="EventMesh.Auth" attribute_id="/attrId::credentialName" attribute_uilabel="" param_key="EMUser"/>
  ... (13 EventMesh-related references total)
</param_references>
```

### parameters.prop -- our (broken) generated ZIP
```
#Tue, 25 Aug 2026 07:47:46 GMT
```

### parameters.propdef -- our (broken) generated ZIP
```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?><parameters><param_references/></parameters>
```

### The exact structural difference
The reference has a `<parameter>` definition **and** a `<reference>`
entry **and** a `parameters.prop` value for every externalized adapter
attribute. Our output had the `{{...}}` text in the `.iflw` but **none** of
the other three pieces -- an incomplete implementation of SAP's
externalized-parameter mechanism, not a wrong one.

### Which compiler function is responsible
`IflowPackager.createParameters(flowDir)` (in
`src/packager/IflowPackager.ts`) -- it unconditionally wrote the empty
baseline shown above, with **no parameter to the `IFlow` model or its
adapters at all**, regardless of what any adapter's properties contained.
This is root cause **B/D combined** from the earlier investigation
template: the AMQP factory correctly writes the placeholder text (A/C were
already ruled out), but nothing downstream ever reads that text back out to
register it as a real parameter.

---

## 3. The fix

### New file: `src/utils/ExternalizedParameters.ts`

A small, focused, reusable utility:
- `extractPlaceholderKey(value)` -- detects a `"{{Name}}"`-shaped string
  and returns `"Name"`.
- `collectExternalizedParameters(flow: IFlow)` -- scans `flow.getSender()`
  and `flow.getReceiver()` and, **only for adapter types this compiler has
  real `parameters.propdef` evidence for**, returns one registration
  `{ paramKey, xsdType, attributeCategory, attributeId }` per
  placeholder-shaped property found.

**Scope is deliberately narrow**: only `'AMQP'` has a populated property/
type map right now (`destinationName`/`host`/`credentialName`/`path`/
`authentication`/`deliveryState` → `xsd:string`; `port`/
`NumberConcurrentProcesses`/`maxRetries`/`queuePrefetch` → `xsd:integer`;
`connectWithTLS`/`disableReplyTo`/`consumeExpiredMessages` → `xsd:boolean`
-- all taken directly from the reference's own 13 `<parameter>` entries).
Every other adapter type (HTTP, JDBC, RFC, JMS, Process Direct, ...) has no
entry in this map, so a `"{{...}}"`-shaped value in any of *their*
properties is **still written through as a literal string with no
parameter registered, exactly as before this change** -- guessing another
adapter's registration format without its own propdef evidence would be
inventing structure, not reproducing it. `attribute_id` uses AMQP's own
evidenced short form (`/attrId::propertyName`) -- confirmed **different**
from RFC/JMS/ProcessDirect's own evidenced long form
(`ctype::AdapterVariant/cname::.../attrId::x`), so this is not a universal
formula to reuse elsewhere without that adapter's own evidence.

### `IflowPackager.createParameters()` rewritten

Now accepts an optional `externalizedParameters: ExternalizedParameter[]`
list:
- **Zero placeholders found** (the overwhelming common case: every
  non-AMQP flow, and every AMQP flow using literal values) → produces the
  **exact original baseline output, byte-for-byte** (verified by a
  dedicated regression test) -- no risk to any existing flow.
- **One or more placeholders found** → writes one `<parameter>` per unique
  parameter name, one `<reference>` per adapter attribute (deduped
  separately, since one parameter name could in principle back multiple
  attributes), and one `KEY=` line per parameter in `parameters.prop`. The
  `parameters.prop` value is **always empty** -- never a real or
  invented-looking infrastructure value; SAP's own Configure > Externalized
  Parameters screen is where a human fills in the real value after import,
  which is exactly the state a freshly generated template flow should be
  in.

### `compile.ts` wiring

`compileToZip()` now calls `collectExternalizedParameters(flow)` and passes
the result into `packager.package(...)`. `IFlow`'s own model, `AmqpAdapter`,
`capabilities.ts`'s required/optional schema, and `validate.ts`'s AD-012
through AD-015 checks are **all unchanged** from commit 9 -- this fix lives
entirely in the packaging layer, exactly where the gap was.

### `capabilities.ts` note updated

Clarified that supplying a `"{{...}}"` placeholder is now sufficient on its
own -- the compiler handles the SAP-side registration automatically; the AI
does not need to do anything beyond using the placeholder string.

---

## 4. Files changed (this package only)

**New**:
- `src/utils/ExternalizedParameters.ts`

**Modified**:
- `src/packager/IflowPackager.ts` -- `createParameters()` rewritten to
  build real parameter definitions/references when needed, unchanged
  baseline otherwise; `package()` gained the optional
  `externalizedParameters` parameter; new small `escapeXml()` helper.
- `src/api/compile.ts` -- `compileToZip()` computes and passes
  `externalizedParameters`.
- `src/api/capabilities.ts` -- AMQP Sender's `notes` field updated to
  describe the automatic registration.
- `test-amqp-regression.ts` -- 2 new scenarios.

**Deliberately not touched**: `AmqpAdapter.ts` itself (its placeholder
acceptance logic from commit 9 was already correct -- the gap was entirely
downstream, in packaging), `validate.ts`'s AD-012 through AD-015 checks,
every other adapter/component type, the sequence-flow fix, the BPMN
mapper/writer layers, the Fiori UI, and the CAP controller/`downloadFlow`
handling.

---

## 5. Testing

Two new scenarios in `test-amqp-regression.ts` (23 total in the file, all
passing):

- **[12]** A flow with `destinationName`/`host`/`port`/`credentialName` all
  set to `"{{...}}"` placeholders: confirms `validate()` succeeds,
  `compileToZip()` succeeds, the ZIP contains both parameter files, and
  then checks **every individual piece** against evidence -- `parameters.prop`
  has a `KEY=` line for each (with an empty default value, never a
  real-looking one), `parameters.propdef` is not the empty baseline, each
  `<parameter>` has the evidence-matched `xsd:string`/`xsd:integer` type,
  and each `<reference>` uses the evidenced short `attribute_id` form with
  `attribute_category` taken from the configured `system` value.
- **[13]** A flow with **literal** (non-placeholder) AMQP values: confirms
  `parameters.propdef` is the **exact original baseline string**, and
  `parameters.prop` matches the exact original bare-timestamp pattern --
  proving this fix introduces zero behavioral change for any flow that
  doesn't use placeholders.

**Full pre-existing regression suite re-run after this change -- zero
regressions**: all 23 AMQP scenarios, `test-jms-regression.ts` (12),
`test-sequence-flow-regression.ts`, `test-rfc-regression.ts` (15),
`test-jdbc-regression.ts`, `test-jdbc-ai-scenarios-regression.ts`,
`test-naming-version-regression.ts`, `test-customer-flow-regression.ts`,
`test-processdirect-regression.ts`,
`test-localintegrationprocess-regression.ts`, `test/run-rt-003-tests.ts`
(9/9), `test/run-mapping-regression-simple.ts` (3/3).

Run with (from `packages/compiler/`):
```
npm run build   # or: npx tsc --noEmit
npx ts-node test-amqp-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-jms-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-sequence-flow-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-rfc-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-jdbc-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-jdbc-ai-scenarios-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-naming-version-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-customer-flow-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-processdirect-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-localintegrationprocess-regression.ts
npx ts-node ../../test/run-rt-003-tests.ts
npx ts-node ../../test/run-mapping-regression-simple.ts
```

